    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d422">

                <pb facs="0431" n="425" sameAs="frus:frus1942v02/pg425"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 (E. W.)/11–742: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Algiers (Cole) to the War Department
                    </hi><anchor n="43" xml:id="d422-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Algiers</hi></placeName>, <date
                            ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content-and-sibling-dates"
                            calendar="gregorian" notAfter="1942-11-05T20:00:00-05:00"
                            notBefore="1942-11-05T12:00:00+01:00" when="1942-11-05"
                        >undated</date>.]<lb/>[Received November 5, 1942—8 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>No. 768. From Murphy. For Leahy. Admiral Darlan arrived Algiers in greatest
                    secrecy by plane today. His son is in dying condition with no hope of recovery.
                    Darlan’s presence here on D–day might be embarrassing but it is believed he will
                    depart before then. [Murphy.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cole</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d422-f1" n="43" type="source" xml:id="d422-f1-content">
